WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.540
Conditional access means authentication and session policy can change based on context.

2
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:19.320
Useful signals may include device posture, location, network context, user risk, sign-in risk, application sensitivity, and whether the user is attempting a privileged action.

3
00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:23.020
The goal is not to make login unpredictable.

4
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:27.760
The goal is to apply stronger checks when the request has higher risk or higher impact.

5
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:30.840
Step-up authentication is a common pattern.

6
00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:42.540
A user may sign in normally for low-risk work, then be asked for stronger proof when accessing a sensitive application, changing security settings, or performing an administrative action.

7
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:52.960
Session controls can limit how long access lasts, require reauthentication, or restrict behavior based on device or risk posture.

8
00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:58.500
These controls should support the business process rather than surprise users without explanation.

9
00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:02.060
Exceptions and break-glass accounts require discipline.

10
00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:08.880
Every organization needs a way to maintain business continuity if the normal identity system or MFA method has a problem.

11
00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:15.220
Those accounts should be few, highly protected, monitored, documented, and tested.

12
00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:23.160
Exceptions for users, devices, locations, or applications should have owners and expiration dates.

13
00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:29.260
Otherwise, conditional access gradually becomes a collection of permanent bypasses.

14
00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:33.460
Risk-based authentication must be transparent to operators.

15
00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:39.400
Policies should be documented, reviewed, tested before broad rollout, and monitored after deployment.

16
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:43.180
Support teams need to know why users are challenged or blocked.

17
00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:48.000
Security teams need logs and alerts that explain policy outcomes.

18
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:56.500
The balance is usability, security, business continuity, and operational support, not one control turned up until work stops.
